14.m:
I saw the guardian who never stumbles, / wandering to and fro along the paths;
clothing himself in those that converge and those that disperse, / he rolls continually within the worlds. —
"He rolls continually within the worlds." Now this great Self is defined by being [sattva]; that is the supreme, that is Brahman, that is the real, that is the water, that is the unmanifest, that is the intangible, that is the formless, that is the tasteless, that is the scentless, that is the immortal, that is the pure; resting in that is the embodied self [bhūtātman]. Some say this is the material cause of beings [bhūtaprakṛti]; reaching the knower of the field [kṣetrajña] through knowledge of it, that field becomes devoid of an individual self. Now this great Self becomes threefold: sattva, rajas, and tamas. But sattva stands purified in the middle, with rajas and tamas on both sides. Desire and hatred are tamas; for one whose [Self] is not understood, as he becomes purified and manifests his sovereign power, it brings about the separateness of the knower of the field. The great Self has intuition [pratibhā] as its mark; tamas has darkness as its mark; knowledge has illumination as its mark; tamas [leads to delusion]; space has certainty as its mark.
